'''trickster''' (trik′stėr), ''n.'' [< ''trick''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ster''.] One who practises tricks; a deceiver; a cheat.

{{smaller block|I'll tell you a Story not much unlike yours, not to go off from Lewis, who us'd to take a Pleasure in tricking ''Tricksters.''
{{right|''N. Bailey'', tr. of Colloquies of Erasmus, I. 434.}}}}

'''trickster''' (trik′stėr), ''v. i.'' [< ''trickster'', ''n.''] To play tricks. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|I like not this lady's tampering and ''trickstering'' with this same Edmund Tressilian.
{{right|''Scott'', Kenilworth, xxxvi.}}}}

'''trick-sword''' (trik′sōrd), ''n.'' A sword made to divide in the middle of the blade.

'''tricksy''' (trik′si), ''a.'' [Also ''tricksey''; < ''trick''<sup>1</sup> + ''-sy'', equiv. to ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. Trickish; cunning; adroit; artful; crafty.

{{smaller block|My ''tricksy'' spirit!
{{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, v. 1. 226.}}
I continued ''tricksy'' and cunning, and was poor without the consolation of being honest.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Vicar, xxvi.}}}}

2. Deceptive; fallacious; illusive; illusory.

{{smaller block|The ''tricksy'' thing [idea] . . . comes and goes, my boy, revealing itself in glimpses which are neither clear enough nor prolonged enough to make that kind of impression on the memory which is necessary to fix it.
{{right|''D. C. Murray'', Weaker Vessel, ii.}}}}

3. Playful; sportive; mischievous.

{{smaller block|Thou little ''tricksy'' Puck!<br>With antic toys so funnily bestuck.
{{right|''Hood'', Parental Ode to my Son.}}}}

4. Trim; dainty; neat; spruce.

{{smaller block|''Trincato'' [It.], . . . spruce, fine, neat, smug, feate, ''tricksie''-trim.
{{right|''Florio'' (ed. 1611).}}
Their little minim forms arrayed}}

